{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1323 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7b91cacfe46aed111683859a277a06d602cbc6055e86a5d9e5dcd8406f8e523d" :edb/db-before-t 1322 :edb/db-after-t 1323 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534635 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:26:34.309Z" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157189 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac {10}{3}$, compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n${8 - 2x,\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\le x \\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139534635 17592186045417 false] [17592186157189 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac {10}{3},$ compute $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n![](images/50/50e7428db936f89942d4c775d2dfe7691b853a9ca729e30c1958cb25b6c1e8d2.svg)" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157189 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{3}^{4}x^{2} \\cdot (8 - 2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{4}(8x^{2} - 2x^{3})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{8x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{2})\\right|_{3}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{67}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{67}{6} - (\\frac{10}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534635 17592186045417 false] [17592186157189 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\textrm E[X^2],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot (8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(8{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{67}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{67}{6}-{(\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157190 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function given below. Given that $\\textrm E[X]\\approx 1.791\\,67$, compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n${\\frac{1}{4},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2, \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3}{4},\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le x < 3,; 0, \\mid otherwise$." 13194139534635 17592186045417 false] [17592186157190 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function given below. Given that $\\textrm E[X]\\approx 1.791\\,67,$ compute $\\textrm{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n![](images/f6/f63c459b8c212033b5dbe5f0c3a4176b7f921788bed579e8615d1a3a3d9b3f17.svg)" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157190 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx + \\int_{2}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}dx + \\int_{2}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3}{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{x^{2}}{4}dx + \\int_{2}^{3}(\\frac{x^{3}}{2} - \\frac{3x^{2}}{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{3}}{12}\\right|_{0}^{2} + \\left.(\\frac{x^{4}}{8} - \\frac{x^{3}}{4})\\right|_{2}^{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 4.04167\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 4.04167 - (1.79167)^{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.83\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534635 17592186045417 false] [17592186157190 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{3}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & ≈4.041\\,67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.041\\,67-(1.791\\,67)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430389 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430392 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430390 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430391 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186157534 1050 840.0 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186158539 1064 "The **variance** of a continuous random variable is the expected squared difference between the variable and its expected value.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the variance is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[(X - E[X])^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\int_{S}(x - E[X])^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn practice, though, it's easier to use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\int_{1}^{3}x \\cdot \\frac{x}{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{x^{2}}{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{3}}{12}\\right|_{1}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{1}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{x}{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{x^{3}}{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{4}}{16}\\right|_{1}^{3} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 5 - (\\frac{13}{6})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{36}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534635 17592186045417 false] [17592186158539 1064 "The **variance** of a continuous random variable is the expected squared difference between the variable and its expected value.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the variance is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn practice, though, it's easier to use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{(\\frac{13}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430365 1080 "5" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430365 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430365 1083 #uuid "0e83b986-ddee-59b4-802a-948fde980775" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430366 1080 "\\frac{7}{16}" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430366 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430366 1083 #uuid "1204147e-9d6e-5e43-8474-be634d7db4f2" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430367 1080 "\\frac{13}{36}" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430367 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430367 1083 #uuid "1efa5566-7842-5b7d-abc6-3035b571fbef" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430368 1080 "4" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430368 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430368 1083 #uuid "2fc2ed58-b9d2-5a83-88bb-b53d2ba1ebbc" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430369 1080 "0.66" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430369 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430369 1083 #uuid "31e8a360-bbb6-5ff2-b1db-6f35ee0fb9fb" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [17592186430370 1080 "0.98" 13194139534635 17592186045417 true] [175921864